            "questionType": "MCQ",
            "passageTitle": "Reading Comprehension 2: Cultural Anthropology & Language",
            "passageText": "Linguistic relativity posits that the structure of a language influences its speakers' worldview and cognition. While early extreme formulations suggested linguistic determinism, contemporary cognitive science emphasizes subtle cognitive framing effects. Language shapes how individuals prioritize spatial orientation, color distinction, and temporal perception without rendering un-encoded concepts completely incomprehensible.",
            "questionText": "[Question 1] Which statement best summarizes the author's stance on contemporary linguistic relativity?",
            "options": [
              "A. Language subtlys frames cognitive prioritization without strictly dictating thought limits.",
              "B. Absolute linguistic determinism has been completely vindicated by modern brain imaging.",
              "C. Spatial orientation is entirely independent of linguistic structures.",
              "D. Un-encoded linguistic concepts can never be comprehended by non-native speakers."
            ],
            "correctAnswer": "A",
            "explanation": "The text states that contemporary cognitive science views language as shaping cognitive prioritization rather than imposing absolute limits.",
            "marksCorrect": 3,
